"# The First Transatlantic Radio Transmission: Key Facts from the Retrieved Sources\n\n## 1. The Traditional Account: Who, When, What\n\n**Who:** Guglielmo Marconi, aged 27, assisted by his company employees **George S. Kemp** (a former Royal Navy officer) and **P. W. Paget** [1][4][5].\n\n**When:** The claimed reception occurred at approximately **12:30 p.m. local time on Thursday, 12 December 1901**, with further apparent receptions logged in the early afternoon (Kemp's diary records signals at about 1:10, 1:20 and 1:30 p.m.; other accounts cite ~2:20 p.m.) [1][2][4]. The timeline of the expedition:\n\n- **6 Dec 1901** — Marconi arrives at St. John's aboard the Allan Line steamer *S.S. Sardinian* [1][4]\n- **9–10 Dec** — Receiving apparatus set up in a room in a disused hospital building on **Signal Hill**, St. John's, Newfoundland, near Cabot Tower [1][4]\n- **11 Dec** — The balloon intended to lift the aerial is torn away and lost in a gale [1][4]\n- **12 Dec** — With a kite holding the aerial aloft, Marconi reports hearing the prearranged signal [1][2][4]\n- **13–14 Dec** — Further tests; the final kite is lost in gales, ending the trials [2][4]\n- **14–15 Dec** — Marconi cables his claim to the press [6]\n\n**What was claimed:** Reception of the Morse letter **\"S\" (three dots)**, transmitted repeatedly from the Marconi Company's high-power spark station at **Poldhu, Cornwall** — a distance of roughly **2,100 miles (~3,400 km)** across the Atlantic [1][2][5][6].\n\n**The equipment:**\n- *Transmitter (Poldhu):* a spark transmitter driven by a **25 kW alternator**, built 1900–01 under **John Ambrose Fleming**, operating at a wavelength of roughly **366 m (~820–850 kHz)**. Critically, its original 400-wire conical aerial (a ring of 20 masts, 200 ft high) had been **destroyed in a storm on 17 September 1901** and replaced by a temporary ~50-wire triangular fan on two masts, reducing radiated power [7][8][9].\n- *Receiver (Signal Hill):* a **coherer** (glass tube with metal filings between silver electrodes) connected to a battery and **telephone earpiece**; a Morse inker was attached but the signals were too faint to record, so reception was **entirely by ear**. Belrose's reconstruction argues a \"self-restoring\" (Italian Navy type) coherer was used [1][3][5].\n- *Aerial:* a single wire roughly **500 ft (150 m) long**, its top end lifted first by balloon (lost 11 Dec), then by **kites** to ~400–500 ft [1][2][4].\n\n---\n\n## 2. Evidence Supporting the Traditional Account\n\n**Witness testimony.** Kemp's diary independently corroborates the later afternoon receptions, recording that he too heard the triple clicks at ~1:10–1:30 p.m. [1][2]. However, the decisive 12:30 p.m. observation was Marconi's alone.\n\n**Marconi's own 1902 confirmation.** In **February 1902**, aboard the *S.S. Philadelphia*, Marconi received Poldhu \"S\" signals **recorded on a Morse inker** — the first documented, verifiable transatlantic reception — at ranges up to **~1,551 miles by day and ~2,099 miles by night** [3][5][7][21]. This is widely treated as the first *confirmed* transatlantic reception.\n\n**Subsequent operational milestones** [1][22][23]:\n- **15 December 1902** — first official transatlantic wireless message transmitted from the Table Head station at **Glace Bay, Cape Breton, Nova Scotia** (Nova Scotia's Lieutenant Governor to King Edward VII)\n- **18 January 1903** — first two-way transatlantic exchange (President Theodore Roosevelt ↔ King Edward VII, via South Wellfleet, MA and Poldhu)\n- **17 October 1907** — first regular **commercial** transatlantic service (Clifden, Ireland ↔ Glace Bay)\n\n**The ionosphere validates the mechanism.** In March 1902, **A. E. Kennelly** and, independently in December 1902, **Oliver Heaviside** postulated a conducting upper-atmospheric layer (the \"Kennelly–Heaviside layer,\" later the ionosphere) *specifically to explain* Marconi's result [16][17]. Its existence was experimentally confirmed by **Appleton & Barnett (1925)** and **Breit & Tuve (1925)** [18]. Marconi's own *Philadelphia* data — signals to ~2,100 miles at night but only ~700 miles by day, a \"sunlight\" effect he explicitly noted — precisely match the day/night absorption asymmetry that D-region ionospheric physics predicts, an internal consistency that strengthens the 1901 report's credibility [7][21].\n\n**Modern technical rehabilitation.** Centenary-era analyses by **J. S. Belrose** (2002, IEEE Marconi Best Paper) and **P. V. S. Bondyopadhyay** (2002) concluded the reception was physically plausible: the operating frequency was ~850 kHz (not very low frequency as early skeptics assumed), radiated power was on the order of 10 kW, December's low winter sun and the 1901–02 **solar minimum** minimized D-layer absorption, and ionospheric reflection could carry the signal. Bondyopadhyay reconstructed the kite-supported receiving antenna from Marconi's notebooks and found its effective height adequate; he also argued (1998) that Marconi's receiver used a **Bose-type self-restoring iron–mercury coherer** [3][9][24]. A **1923 re-enactment** by the Marconi Company — a replica 1901 Poldhu spark transmitter, signals reportedly received at Signal Hill on replica equipment with a kite aerial — is cited as supporting feasibility, though primary documentation of the 1923 event is sparse [29].\n\n---\n\n## 3. Evidence Challenging the Traditional Account\n\n**The single-witness problem.** The only listeners were Marconi, Kemp, and Paget. No paper-tape record exists (the inker could not record the weak signals), the receiver was kept secret for commercial reasons, and Marconi declined independent testing. The decisive observation was Marconi's alone, listening for a prearranged letter whose content he knew in advance [1][5][13][14].\n\n**The physics of daytime MF propagation.** This is the strongest technical objection [9][10][12]:\n- The **ground wave** is negligible at 3,500 km (efficient only to ~1,000–1,500 km over seawater)\n- A single **E-layer hop** covers at most ~2,300–2,400 km, so two hops (doubling absorption) would be needed\n- **Daytime D-region absorption** at 820 kHz is severe (scaling ~1/f²); daytime 820 kHz skywave is normally usable only over a few hundred to ~1,000 km — never ~3,500 km\n- Centennial-era **link budgets** put the received signal roughly **30–100 dB below** what a coherer required, even under optimistic assumptions (winter, solar minimum, high radiated power)\n- **Watson's rigorous diffraction theory (1918–19)** later confirmed ground-wave attenuation was far too severe, and the Kennelly–Heaviside layer was itself an implicit admission that no known mechanism explained the claim [16][17][19]\n\n**The atmospherics/expectation-bias critique.** At ~1 MHz, **atmospheric noise** (distant lightning sferics propagated thousands of km) dominates; a coherer fires on *any* sufficiently strong impulse and cannot distinguish a keyed spark signal from random sferic clusters. Three faint clicks are statistically indistinguishable from random static groups — and Marconi knew the schedule and the letter in advance [9][10][11]. Belrose's earlier (1995) assessment concluded Marconi most likely **heard atmospheric noise**, and that the first verifiable transatlantic reception came in 1902 [3][6].\n\n**The crippled transmitter.** Poldhu's main aerial had been destroyed three months earlier; the temporary replacement was inefficient, the radiated power likely only a few kW at most, and the transmitted frequency was never precisely documented [7][8][9].\n\n**Marconi's own contradictory data.** The 1902 *Philadelphia* tests — in which daylight reception faded beyond ~700 miles — implicitly undercut a *daytime* 3,500-km reception in 1901 [7][21].\n\n**Contemporary skepticism.** Leading researchers (Oliver Lodge; earlier W. H. Preece) argued Hertzian waves travel in straight lines and could not follow the Earth's curvature; the technical press questioned a claim made without receiver details or test protocol; cable-telegraph interests dismissed it as publicity [6][14][28]. The **Anglo-American Telegraph Company** (holder of Newfoundland's exclusive cable concession) threatened legal action, forcing Marconi to leave Newfoundland by January 1902 and shift operations to Nova Scotia [4][22].\n\n---\n\n## 4. Historians' Assessments\n\n- **W. J. Baker** (*A History of the Marconi Company*, 1970) — broadly sympathetic; treats the claim as credible mainly because the rigorous 1902 tests appeared to vindicate it, while conceding the storm-damaged transmitter and absence of independent witnesses [7]\n- **Sungook Hong** (*Wireless: From Marconi's Black-Box to the Audion*, 2001) — the most critical: the 1901 test was not a controlled experiment but a **publicity/entrepreneurial demonstration**; because of Marconi's \"black-box\" secrecy there was no verification, and the meaning of the faint clicks was established rhetorically, with confirmation supplied only retrospectively by 1902 [14]\n- **Marc Raboy** (*Marconi: The Man Who Networked the World*, 2016) — treats the reception as **plausible but ultimately unverifiable**, detailing the contradictory testimony, crippled transmitter, and failed recorder; the event's importance is as a founding media event — \"whether or not\" the S was heard, the announcement itself changed history [13]\n- **Hugh Aitken** (*Syntony and Spark*, 1976) — cautious; emphasizes the lack of verification at the time [15]\n- **Susan Douglas** (1987) — documents skepticism in the contemporary technical press [28]\n- **J. S. Belrose** — the leading modern *technical* participant in the debate; his 1995 paper was skeptical (atmospheric noise), while his centenary (2001–02) modeling argued feasibility. Later engineers have countered in both directions, so the technical debate remains unsettled [3][9]\n\n**Consensus position:** The 1901 reception can never be conclusively confirmed — it was a single-witness, unrecorded event amid transmitter problems. The first *scientifically verified* transatlantic reception is generally dated to the **February 1902** *Philadelphia* experiments, the first official messages to **December 1902** (Glace Bay), and dependable commercial service to **17 October 1907** [5][13][14][22].\n\n---\n\n## 5. Alternative and Earlier Claimants\n\nNo rival actually claimed transatlantic reception before Marconi, though several hold priority for key components [24][25][26][27]:\n\n- **Nikola Tesla** — tuned-circuit patents (1897/1900) and Colorado Springs experiments (1899–1900), but never demonstrated transoceanic Hertzian-wave telegraphy. The **1943 U.S. Supreme Court decision** (*Marconi Wireless Tel. Co. v. United States*, 320 U.S. 1) voided Marconi's 1904 tuning patent as anticipated by Tesla, Lodge, and Stone — a patent-validity ruling, *not* a declaration that Tesla invented radio [25][26]\n- **Oliver Lodge** — first tuning/syntony patent (1897); public Hertzian-wave Morse demonstrations (1894), but short-range only; never claimed transatlantic reception [27]\n- **Alexander Popov** — 1895 lightning detector, 1896 short-range transmission, 1900 ~45 km rescue link; no transatlantic claim [27]\n- **Jagadish Chandra Bose** — 1895 millimetre-wave demonstrations and the self-recovering coherer that Bondyopadhyay argues Marconi's 1901 receiver actually used [24]\n- **Reginald Fessenden** — first genuine **two-way** transatlantic telegraphy (Brant Rock, MA ↔ Machrihanish, Scotland, early 1906) and the contested first voice broadcast (Dec 1906) [3][15]\n\n---\n\n## 6. Bottom Line\n\n| Question | Answer per the retrieved sources |\n|---|---|\n| Who? | Guglielmo Marconi, with G. S. Kemp and P. W. Paget |\n| When? | 12 December 1901, ~12:30 p.m. local time (further receptions early afternoon) |\n| Where/from where? | Signal Hill, St. John's, Newfoundland; signal from Poldhu, Cornwall (~3,400 km) |\n| What signal? | Morse letter \"S\" (three dots), received by ear through a coherer; no tape record |\n| Strongest support | Kemp's corroboration; the ionosphere as a viable mechanism (winter solar minimum); 1902 *Philadelphia* recorded receptions; 1902–07 operational milestones; Belrose/Bondyopadhyay feasibility modeling |\n| Strongest challenges | Sole-witness, unrecorded observation with expectation bias; 30–100 dB link-budget deficit for daytime 820 kHz; atmospherics indistinguishable from three clicks; crippled post-storm Poldhu aerial; Marconi's own 1902 daytime range limit (~700 miles) |\n| Scholarly verdict | Plausible but unverifiable; first *claimed* reception 1901, first *confirmed* reception February 1902, first official messages December 1902, first reliable commercial service 17 October 1907 |\n\n### Consolidated Source List\n[1] G. S. Kemp, Signal Hill diary (Dec 1901), repr. in W. J. Baker, *A History of the Marconi Company* (1970) · [2] G. Marconi, Nobel Lecture (1909) and 1902 accounts · [3] J. S. Belrose, \"Fessenden and Marconi…,\" IEEE Conf. on 100 Years of Radio (1995) · [4] Heritage Newfoundland and Labrador (heritage.nf.ca) · [5] IEEE Engineering and Technology History Wiki, \"First Transatlantic Radio Reception (1901)\" · [6] Contemporary press (NYT; The Times), Dec 1901 · [7] W. J. Baker, *A History of the Marconi Company* (1970) · [8] J. A. Fleming, *The Principles of Electric Wave Telegraphy* (1906) · [9] J. S. Belrose, centenary re-analyses (*IEEE Canadian Review* 2002; *Radio Science Bulletin* 2001–02) · [10] Sarkar et al., *History of Wireless* (2006) · [11] ITU-R Rec. P.372 · [12] K. Davies, *Ionospheric Radio* (1990) · [13] M. Raboy, *Marconi: The Man Who Networked the World* (2016) · [14] S. Hong, *Wireless: From Marconi's Black-Box to the Audion* (2001) · [15] H. G. J. Aitken, *Syntony and Spark* (1976); *The Continuous Wave* (1985) · [16] A. E. Kennelly, *Electrical World & Engineer* (Mar 1902) · [17] O. Heaviside, \"Telegraphy,\" *Encyclopaedia Britannica*, 10th ed. (1902) · [18] Appleton & Barnett, *Nature* (1925); Breit & Tuve, *Phys. Rev.* (1926) · [19] G. N. Watson, *Proc. R. Soc. A* (1918–19) · [20] J. A. Ratcliffe, *Electronics & Power* (1974) · [21] Marconi, *S.S. Philadelphia* test reports (Feb–Mar 1902) · [22] The Canadian Encyclopedia; Parks Canada (Marconi NHS) · [23] Encyclopaedia Britannica · [24] P. V. S. Bondyopadhyay, *Proc. IEEE* 86(1) (1998); IEEE AP Magazine centenary series (2002) · [25] *Marconi Wireless Tel. Co. v. United States*, 320 U.S. 1 (1943) · [26] W. B. Carlson, *Tesla: Inventor of the Electrical Age* (2013) · [27] G. R. M. Garratt, *The Early History of Radio* (1994) · [28] S. J. Douglas, *Inventing American Broadcasting* (1987) · [29] Poldhu Amateur Radio Club / Marconi Centre histories · [30] Tesla patents 645,576 & 649,621; *Colorado Springs Notes*"